        {
          "type": "aside_text",
          "bbox": [
            0.087,
            0.658,
            0.108,
            0.862
          ],
          "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.084,
            0.883,
            0.144
          ],
          "content": "The plants and animals that live in water are said to live in **aquatic** habitats. Some examples of aquatic habitats are ponds, lakes, rivers, and oceans."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.143,
            0.882,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": "Some animals, such as frogs, can live in water as well as on land. These are called **amphibians**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.183,
            0.883,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "What would happen if the habitat of a plant or an animal is damaged? What would happen if a goat does not get grass to eat? Can a fish survive without water?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.242,
            0.884,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "Check with your parents, grandparents, and neighbours to know about the plants, birds, insects or any other animal they used to see frequently in their childhood but do not see as often now. These changes often happen when habitats are damaged. The damage to habitats of plants and animals results in loss of their homes, food, and other resources. This leads to the loss of biodiversity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.441,
            0.884,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": "The population of the Bengal Tiger, Cheetah, and Great Indian Bustard has declined in India due to loss of natural habitats caused by human activities. The Government of India has initiated several projects to conserve our biodiversity. ‘Project Tiger’ was initiated in 1973 to protect the declining population of the Bengal Tiger. The ‘Cheetah Reintroduction Project’ was initiated in 2022 to restore the population of the Cheetah. Similarly, habitats of the Great Indian Bustards have been declared as Protected areas in the states of Gujarat, Rajasthan and Maharashtra."
        },
        {
